{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a significant noble figure in the Levant during the twelfth century who served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. His life was defined by complex political maneuvering within the Crusader states and his relationship with the powerful Ayyubid leader Saladin. Raymond held the County of Tripoli as a vassal to the Kingdom of Jerusalem yet often acted with considerable autonomy due to his vast lands and military strength. He inherited the title at a young age after the death of his father Raymond II who was assassinated by the Hashashin sect. The young ruler faced immediate challenges from internal factions and external threats that would shape his entire career.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond spent his youth in Antioch under the guardianship of Prince Bohemond III before he returned to Tripoli to claim his inheritance. His regency lasted several years while he matured into a capable administrator and military commander. He managed to stabilize his county through diplomatic marriages and strategic alliances with neighboring powers. His ability to maintain peace between the fractious Crusader lords earned him respect among his peers despite his occasionally controversial decisions. The political landscape of the region demanded constant vigilance against Muslim forces that sought to reclaim the coastal territories.\n\n*Role in the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\nAs one of the most powerful barons in the Holy Land Raymond frequently intervened in the affairs of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He served as regent for the young King Baldwin V and acted as a key advisor to King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond often found himself at odds with the faction led by Guy of Lusidian and his wife Sibylla. This internal strife weakened the kingdom at a critical time when Saladin was consolidating power in Egypt and Syria. His 